        "side": "new",
        "severity": "high",
        "category": "bug",
        "title": "{%endcomment%} / {%endverbatim%} without spaces no longer close raw blocks",
        "explanation": "The old lexer compared token_string[2:-2].strip() to 'endverbatim' (and the comment tag used skip_past on stripped contents), so '{%endverbatim%}' and '{%endcomment%}' were valid closers. _comment_end_re and _verbatim_end_re now require at least one whitespace after '{%' ([^\\S\\n]+). A template such as '{% comment %}x{%endcomment%}rest' or '{%verbatim%}{{ a }}{%endverbatim%}' (debug mode, where _raw_end_tag_re strips and detects the opener) never leaves raw mode. The rest of the template becomes text and parsing fails with an unclosed comment/verbatim TemplateSyntaxError. Templates that used to work now break. The named-verbatim regex uses [^\\S\\n]* instead, so the two kinds of closer are inconsistent.",
        "suggestion": "Use [^\\S\\n]* (zero or more) after BLOCK_TAG_START in _comment_end_re and _verbatim_end_re, matching the named-verbatim regex and the old strip()-based comparison. Add tests for '{%endcomment%}' and '{%verbatim%}...{%endverbatim%}'.",
        "evidence": "r\"%s[^\\S\\n]+endcomment[^\\S\\n]*%s\"",
        "confidence": 0.85,
        "id": "7c2b0d78f375",
        "hunk_id": "django/template/base.py#1",
        "model": "claude-opus-5-5",
        "tier": 3,
        "escalated_from": [],
        "also_reported_by": []
      },
      {
        "file": "django/template/base.py",
        "start_line": 491,
        "end_line": 496,
        "side": "new",
        "severity": "high",
        "category": "bug",
        "title": "Fast path misses '{%verbatim%}' so verbatim content is parsed as template",
        "explanation": "_raw_start_re requires whitespace after '{%' (\\s+), so a template using '{%verbatim%}...{%endverbatim%}' takes the plain tag_re.split path in the non-debug Lexer. The verbatim body is then lexed as real VAR/BLOCK tokens. '{%verbatim%}{{ x }}{%endverbatim%}' renders '' (VerbatimNode renders the nodelist with an empty Context) instead of '{{ x }}', and block tags inside can raise syntax errors. The old lexer treated this as verbatim. DebugLexer always uses _tag_re_split, which does detect the opener (via strip/split), so debug and non-debug now behave differently for the same template.",
        "suggestion": "Make _raw_start_re accept optional whitespace (e.g. r\"%s\\s*(?:comment|verbatim)\\b\"), since false positives are harmless here.",
        "evidence": "if self._raw_start_re.search(self.template_string) is None:",
        "confidence": 0.8,
